56-///
57-/// This is what makes [`members_without_functions`] a per-member check rather
58-/// than a per-module one. The check it replaced asked whether *any* builtin
59-/// started with `<module>_`, which one stub answers for the whole module:
60-/// `list_map` made `list` look complete while `list.filter` and `list.reduce`
61-/// — two names nothing implements — counted as members of it, and dropping
62-/// `filter` from the registry entirely would have left the gate green. Every
63-/// member is named here, so a member the language has is a line in this table,
64-/// and a member that resolves to nothing is one name rather than a whole
65-/// namespace nobody looked at.
66-///
67-/// It is also the surface the language enforces rather than only documents, and
68-/// it is enforced on **both** of the paths a dotted call can take:
69-///
70-/// - a flat-backed member ([`module_member_name`]) unwraps `module_member` to
71-///   `member` only for a pair this table holds;
